.TH CJSON_GET 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cjson_get \- navigate to a DOM node with a dot-separated path
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

cjson cjson_get(cjson root, const char *path);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Walks from root through the components of path and returns the node
that the last component addresses.

path is a sequence of components separated by dots.  When the
current node is a CJSON_LIST, a component made of '#' and one or
more decimal digits addresses an array element by its zero-based
index.  In every other case the whole component is a literal object
key, with the '#' as part of the key.  A bare '#' with no digits on
a CJSON_LIST node is an error and the macro returns NULL.

A NULL root gives NULL, and a NULL or empty path gives root itself.
An empty component addresses nothing, so the macro gives NULL for
it; a leading dot, a trailing dot and two dots in a row (as in
"a..b") all make an empty component.

Escape sequences in path components:
  \e.   -> a literal '.' in the key (not treated as a path separator)
  \e\e   -> a literal '\e' in the key
A '\e' before any other character is kept as it is.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
root   Root cjson node, usually a CJSON_DICTIONARY or a CJSON_LIST.
path   Dot-separated path string, for example
       "users.#0.address.city".
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A cjson handle to the target node, which the caller does not own.
NULL when a component along the path is missing, or when the current
node's type doesn't allow the requested step.  A path of 256 bytes or
more is first copied with root's allocator, and the macro also
returns NULL when that copy fails.  A shorter path needs no
allocation.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The returned handle becomes invalid once the caller changes or
destroys the tree.  Do not free it.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cjson city = cjson_get(doc, "users.#0.address.city");
if (cjson_type(city) == CJSON_STRING)
    printf("%s\en", cjson_str_val(city));

/* '#0' in an object context is a literal key, not an index */
cjson v = cjson_get(doc, "#0");
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_set (3),
.BR cjson_type (3)
